window.__NOTE_CONTENT = "<p>l</p>\n<p>Intellectual Property</p>\n<p>This is a category of intangible rights protecting commercially valuable products of the</p>\n<p>human intellect. Unlike real property andpersonal property, which is often protected by</p>\n<p>means of physical security devices (such as fences and other enclosures), intellectual</p>\n<p>property is essentially protected by sets of enforceablelegal rights granted to “owners”</p>\n<p>or “holders.” These legal rights are intended to solve the economic problem described</p>\n<p>by KennethArrow as the “incomplete appropriable of knowledge.</p>\n<p>Due to the fact that Intellectual Property is intangible and typically easy to copy and</p>\n<p>transport, it is difficult for business enterprises to capture the full value of investments</p>\n<p>in it (i.e., competitors can easily appropriate it). Intellectual Property rights are efforts</p>\n<p>to solve this incomplete ‘appropriability’ problem.</p>\n<p>Brief Comments on IP</p>\n<p>Each time we buy such ‘protected’ items, a part of what we pay goes back to the owner</p>\n<p>as recompense for the time, money, effort and thought they put in creation of the</p>\n<p>work. This has resulted over many years in the development of industries suchas the</p>\n<p>music industry growing worldwide and encouraging new talent to produce more and</p>\n<p>more original ideas and articles.</p>\n<p>Let’s begin with a more formal definition of intellectual property, so as to build a solid</p>\n<p>foundation for later explanations of the various types of intellectual property, and then</p>\n<p>the relevant</p>\n<p>governing international treaties. The best place to start is with a consideration of the</p>\n<p>meaning of the word property. The outstanding features that most types of property</p>\n<p>share are that the owner of the property is free to use it as she /he wishes provided the</p>\n<p>use is not against the law, and to exclude others from so using that owned item of</p>\n<p>property.</p>\n<p>Now the term “intellectual property” is reserved for types ofproperty that result</p>\n<p>from creations of the human mind, the intellect. Interestingly, the term intellectual</p>\n<p>property in the Convention Establishing the World Intellectual PropertyOrganization,</p>\n<p>or “WIPO”, does not have a more formal definition. The States that drafted the</p>\n<h4 id=\"convention-chose-to-offer-an-inclusivelist-of-the-rights-as-relating-to\">Convention chose to offer an inclusivelist of the rights as relating to:</h4>\n<p>“Literary,artistic and scientific works; performances of performingartists, phonograms,</p>\n<p>and broadcasts; inventions in all fields of human endeavor; scientific discoveries;</p>\n<p>industrial designs; trademarks, service marks, and commercial names and designations;</p>\n<p>protection against unfair competition; and “all otherrights resulting from intellectual</p>\n<p>activity in the industrial, scientific, literary or artistic fields.” (Convention Establishing</p>\n<p>the World Intellectual Property Organization, signed at Stockholm on July 14, 1967;</p>\n<p>Article 2, viii)</p>\n<p>For various administrative and historical reasons, intellectual property is usually dealt</p>\n<h4 id=\"with-under-the-following-main-headings\">with under the following main headings:</h4>\n<p>1) Literary, artistic and scientific works e.g. books. Protection ofthis property is</p>\n<p>governed by laws concerning Copyright.</p>\n<p>2) Performances, broadcasts e.g. concerts. Protection of this property is governed</p>\n<p>by laws concerning Copyright’s RelatedRights.</p>\n<p>3) Inventions e.g. a new form of jet engine. Protection ofinventions is covered by</p>\n<p>laws concerning Patents.</p>\n<p>4) Industrial designs e.g. the shape of a soft drinks bottle. Industrial Designs may</p>\n<p>be protected by its own specialized laws, or those of Industrial Property or</p>\n<p>Copyright.</p>\n<p>5) Trademarks, service marks and commercial names and designations e.g. logos or</p>\n<p>names for a product with unique geographical origin, such as Champagne.</p>\n<p>Protection is normally available under various laws. In this course thelaws are</p>\n<p>covered within the Trademark module.</p>\n<p>6) Protection against unfair competition. E.g. false claims against a competitor or</p>\n<p>imitating a competitor with a view todeceive the customer.</p>\n<p>Why do Intellectual Property Rights Matter?</p>\n<p>The first reason is that it is both just and appropriate that the person putting in the</p>\n<p>work and effort into an intellectual creation has some benefit as a result of this</p>\n<p>endeavor. The second reason is that by giving protection to intellectual property many</p>\n<p>such endeavors are encouraged and industries based on such work cangrow, as people</p>\n<p>see that such works bring financial return. Intellectual property rights may also help to</p>\n<p>extend protection to such things as the unwritten and unrecorded cultural expression</p>\n<p>of many developing countries, generally known as folklore. With</p>\n<p>such protection they may be exploited to the benefit of thecountry and cultures of</p>\n<p>origin.</p>\n<p>The reason for States to enact national legislation, and to join as signatories to either</p>\n<p>(or both) regional or international treaties governing intellectual property rights include:</p>\n<p>- To provide incentive towards various creative endeavors of the mind by</p>\n<p>offering protections;</p>\n<p>- To give such creators official recognition;</p>\n<p>- To create repositories of vital information;</p>\n<p>- To facilitate the growth of both domestic industry or culture,and international</p>\n<p>trade, through the treaties offering multi- lateral protection.</p>\n<p>Copyright law</p>\n<p>This is the branch of the Intellectual Property, which provides a body of rules that</p>\n<p>govern copyright with the aim of fostering growth in the creative and cultural industries,</p>\n<p>encouraging humancreativity, inspiring new talent and overall creating an enjoyable and</p>\n<p>educative environment for mankind.</p>\n<p>Copyright is a bundle of exclusive legal rights concerned with the protection of literary</p>\n<p>and artistic works. The aim of copyright is toreward authors for their intellectual efforts</p>\n<p>and at the same time promote science, culture and the arts.</p>\n<p>According to Article 2 of the Berne Convention for the Protection of Literary and</p>\n<p>Artistic Works, which is the oldest multilateral agreement in the area of copyright, “the</p>\n<p>expression ‘literary and artistic works’ shall include every production in the literary,</p>\n<p>scientific and artistic domain, whatever may be the mode or form of its expression, such</p>\n<p>as books, pamphlets and other writings; lectures, addresses, sermons and other works</p>\n<p>of the same nature; dramatic or dramatico-musical works; choreographic works and</p>\n<p>entertainments in dumb show; musical compositions with or without words;</p>\n<p>cinematographic works to which are assimilated works expressed by a process</p>\n<p>analogous to cinematography; works of drawing, painting, architecture, sculpture,</p>\n<p>engraving and lithography; photographic works, to which are assimilated works</p>\n<p>expressed by a process analogous tophotography; works of applied art; illustrations,</p>\n<p>maps, plans,sketches and three-dimensional works relative to geography, topography,</p>\n<p>architecture or science. […] Translations, adaptations, arrangements of music and</p>\n<p>other alternations of a literary or artistic work shall be protected as original works</p>\n<p>without prejudice to the copyright in the original work […] Collections of literary or</p>\n<p>artistic works, such as encyclopedias and anthologies which, by reason of the selection</p>\n<p>and arrangement oftheir contents constitute intellectual creations shall be protected</p>\n<p>as such, without prejudice to the copyright in each of the works forming part of such</p>\n<p>collections.”</p>\n<p>This is quite some list, but it is not exhaustive. It only refers tothe most characteristic</p>\n<p>examples of copyrightable subject matter. Any creation of the mind can attract</p>\n<p>copyright protection as long</p>\n<p>5</p>\n<p>as it meets the requirements of the relevant national law. For example, new technology</p>\n<p>or technology-based works are also protected, such as computer programs, databases</p>\n<p>and multimediaworks.</p>\n<p>So the term “literary and artistic works” can be explained in termsof quite a long list</p>\n<p>but within that term it is worth looking at the term ‘works’ in more detail. It is important</p>\n<p>to remember that copyright protects ‘works’. Works represent the expression of</p>\n<p>thoughts and ideas. Ideas and thoughts as such are not protected. That would</p>\n<p>unjustifiably restrict freedom of expression,free access to information and free access</p>\n<p>to raw material, which is essential for the advancement of culture.</p>\n<p>So, if I conceive the idea of writing a novel about a slave’s life in Ancient Rome, this</p>\n<p>idea can be freely copied by anyone. It is onlywhen I express my story in words, either</p>\n<p>on paper or otherwise, that l get protection for that particular form of expression.</p>\n<p>You should, however, bear in mind that copyright protection is territorial in nature.</p>\n<p>That means that a work will only attract copyright protection if it meets the legal</p>\n<p>requirements of the copyright law of the country where protection is sought. Each</p>\n<p>author has a separate copyright in each country and when his/hercopyright in one</p>\n<p>country is exhausted it does not mean that all copyrights in all countries are equally</p>\n<p>exhausted.</p>\n<p>There are two types of rights under copyright: economic rights, which allow the owner</p>\n<p>of rights to derive financial reward from the use of his works by others, and moral</p>\n<p>rights, which allow the</p>\n<p>6</p>\n<p>author to take certain actions to preserve the personal link between himself and the</p>\n<p>work. Now listen to the next audio segment and try to distinguish the various economic</p>\n<p>rights that are described.</p>\n<p>Copyright law in Uganda</p>\n<p>In Uganda, it is regulated by the Copyright and NeighboringRights Act No. 19 of 2006</p>\n<p>(CNRA) (which repealed and replaced the Copyright Act Cap 215 of 1964), as well as</p>\n<p>the Copyright and Neighboring Rights Regulations, 2010.</p>\n<p>The Act applies to works created by Uganda citizens or residents, works first published</p>\n<p>in Uganda irrespective of author’s nationalor resident of any member state to WIPO,</p>\n<p>ARIPO and UNESCO (Sec 3,81 and the 1st schedule of the Act), or a work first</p>\n<p>published in a member state to the TRIPS. In British Northrop V Texteam, it was held</p>\n<p>that publication takes place wherever the publisher invites the public to acquire copies</p>\n<p>not where the copiesare received. Subsistence of copyright has two concerning factors;</p>\n<p>Personal and Territorial criterion. The personal criterion isbased on the nationality or</p>\n<p>residence of the author or maker (qualified person). It does not matter where the work</p>\n<p>as published or made. Territorial criterion is based on the place of publication or making</p>\n<p>of the work. It must have been first published or made in the territory. The status of</p>\n<p>the author or maker is irrelevant. (See S.3, 81 Schedule I and II CNRA).</p>\n<p>7</p>\n<p>There has been a commendable effort by government exhibited inthe enactment of the</p>\n<p>CNRA 2006 and CNRR 2010 for prior to that; the old law had been overtaken by</p>\n<p>modern circumstances.</p>\n<p>Copyright</p>\n<p>This is that legal right granted to authors and artists to protect expressive works against</p>\n<p>unauthorized reproduction or distribution by third parties.</p>\n<p>Expressive works are broadly defined, and include such things as books, films, music</p>\n<p>recordings and computer software. There is infact no express limit on what material</p>\n<p>might be considered to embody protectable artistic expression. However, copyright</p>\n<p>does not extend to functional works or ideas.</p>\n<p>This principle is often referred to as the “idea-expression dichotomy,” with the “idea”</p>\n<p>excluded from copyright protection. Under the TRIPS Agreement, the minimum term</p>\n<p>of copyright protection is the life of the author plus 50 years. However, in a number of</p>\n<p>places, including the United States and the European Union, the duration of copyright</p>\n<p>has been extended to the life of the author plus 70 years as the law may provide</p>\n<p>according to different jurisdictions.</p>\n<p>Subject matter of copyright protection</p>\n<p>Subject matter of copyright protection includes every</p>\n<p>i)      Literary works</p>\n<p>ii)     Scientific</p>\n<p>iii)    Artistic works</p>\n<p>iv)     Musical works</p>\n<p>v)      Maps and technical drawings photographic works</p>\n<p>vi)     Computer programs</p>\n<p>vii)    Sound recordings etc.</p>\n<p>Literary</p>\n<p>S 5 CNRA provides for literary works and S.2 CNRA defines literary works to include</p>\n<p>novels, stories poetic work text books, and everything expressed in print or writing.</p>\n<p>Copyright law does not extend to Short combination of words, simple phrases,</p>\n<p>speeches, signatures and slogans lacking any literarycompositions are not protected no</p>\n<p>matter how unique they may be.</p>\n<p>In the case of Hollinrakes vs. Truswell, Davey J held that Literary works should be</p>\n<p>something that afforded information, instructionsor pleasure in the form of literary</p>\n<p>enjoyment. Names Tittle and simple phrases have been rejected.</p>\n<p>Francis Day and Hunter Ltd Vs Twentieth Central case it was heldthat; Names Tittle and</p>\n<p>simple phrases have been rejected.</p>\n<p>In Green vs. Broadcasting Corporation of Newzland (1989) 2 ALLER 1056 it was held</p>\n<p>that there was no work.</p>\n<p>Exxon Corporation and Ors vs. Exxon Insurance ConsultantsInternational Ltd. (1982)</p>\n<p>Ch 119 it was held the word Exxon couldnot amount to work. On appeal CA held that</p>\n<p>original literary workwas a composite expression to be construed as such and not as</p>\n<p>three separate words.</p>\n<p>Implied exempted works</p>\n<p>Some categories of literary works may be excluded by implications</p>\n<p>S.7 excludes public benefit work from protection</p>\n<p>S.7(1) states that that the right to protection shall not extend to the following works;</p>\n<p>Students may consult the Act for more details.</p>\n<p>- An enactment           including       an    Act    of   Parliament       Decree,</p>\n<p>Statutory Instruments etc.</p>\n<p>-   Court decisions</p>\n<p>-   A report made by the committee, Commission of inquiry,</p>\n<p>-   News of the day</p>\n<p>-   Law reports</p>\n<p>-   Written laws etc.</p>\n<p>In University of London Press vs. Universal Tutorial Press (1961) 2Ch 601 the issue was</p>\n<p>whether examination papers fell within the meaning of literary works. it was held that,</p>\n<p>literary works are notlimited to only works of literature but include all works expressed</p>\n<p>in print or writing other than musical or dramatic works. That copyright therefore</p>\n<p>subsisted in examination papers as original</p>\n<p>literary works within the meaning of the Act and copyright was vested in the examiners.</p>\n<p>Protection is conferred irrespective of the quality of the works seesection 4 (1) CNRA</p>\n<p>literary works is tested against the concept of sufficient skill, labor, and judgment.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-4-3-cnra-it-s-not-enough-for-work-to-be-expressed-in-writing-or-print-if-there\">Section 4 (3) CNRA it’s not enough for work to be expressed in writing or print if there</h3>\n<p>is no evidence of any skill, labor applied tocreate the work.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-5-2-literary-works-also-cover-derivative-secondary-works-on-existing\">Section 5 (2) literary works also cover derivative/ secondary works on existing</h3>\n<p>resources provided that in turn involves literary,skill labor and judgment. In Byrne vs.</p>\n<p>Statist Company (1914) I KB 622 it was held that a translation may itself constitute</p>\n<p>infringement but was an original literary works in which theplaintiff was the author, the</p>\n<p>plaintiff was the owner of the copyright therein.</p>\n<p>see Rose vs. Information Service Ltd (1987) FSR254</p>\n<p>Musical Works</p>\n<p>Many laws do not define musical works. Copyright Act of Zimbabwe defines it as a</p>\n<p>“Work consisting of music but does not include any work oraction intends to be</p>\n<p>sung, spoken, or performed with the music.”</p>\n<p>To prove music, sound recording, musical composition, lyrics composition music</p>\n<p>performers must be established.</p>\n<p>Musical works should be defined in the national laws.</p>\n<p>Therefore, Section 5 of the CRNA defines musical works as “including musical of</p>\n<p>exclusively of any words or action intends tobe sung, spoken, or performed with the</p>\n<p>music. Separate copyright exists in the lyrics and the song itself; the lyrics constitute</p>\n<p>literary works and the song (tune) is protected as musical works. Stuart vs. Barret 1994</p>\n<p>EMLR 488; it was held that the contribution by a drummer in a rock band to a song was</p>\n<p>recognized as part of the musical works and was therefore co- author.</p>\n<p>Chappell &amp; Co. Ltd vs. Redwood HOL held the first owner of the words was the owner</p>\n<p>of the copyright.</p>\n<p>The distinction came up in case of C.F.A O VS. Archibald where itwas held by the</p>\n<p>supreme court of Ghana that mere words or verses with no intelligible musical notation</p>\n<p>indicating the melody did not qualify to be called musical works.</p>\n<p>Artistic work</p>\n<p>Artistic works includes paintings, drawings, maps, charts, photographs, engravings,</p>\n<p>sculptures or artistic craftsmanship. Artistic merit and subject matter are irrelevant.</p>\n<p>Artistic works are protected irrespective artistic quality and may extend tophotography,</p>\n<p>sculpture, work of architecture etc.</p>\n<p>Creation Records vs. News Group News (TRL29.4.97. It was held that the process of</p>\n<p>arranging objects and members of a group to form a scene comprised in the photograph</p>\n<p>could be considered as</p>\n<p>sculpture or a collage under the act or as a work of artistic craftsmanship. In Metix (UK)</p>\n<p>vs. Maughan Plastics (1997) FSR718, it was held that something which had a transient</p>\n<p>existence might nevertheless be work of sculpture.</p>\n<p>Dramatic works</p>\n<p>These include plays, opera, mime recitation pieces etc. Dramatic works are not defined</p>\n<p>by the Act but include works of Dance and mime; in Tate vs. Full Brook (1908) 11 KB</p>\n<h3 id=\"821-there-was-an-alleged-infringement-of-a-dramatic-piece-it-was-held-that-acts-relating\">821. There was an alleged infringement of a dramatic piece. It was held that acts relating</h3>\n<p>to dramatic copyright contemplate as the subject ofprotection under them, something</p>\n<p>which can be printed or published. Therefore, where there is a dramatic piece in which</p>\n<p>there is copyright as regards the verbal composition, is substanceentirely different from</p>\n<p>another dramatic piece alleged toconstitute an infringement of tat copyright, the mere</p>\n<p>fact that accessorial matters such as scenic effects, make up of actors, “business” in the</p>\n<p>latter piece, as performed are similar to those employed in the performance of the</p>\n<p>former will not constitute infringement of the copyright therein such matters, taken by</p>\n<p>themselves.</p>\n<p>To qualify as dramatic work, it must be a work of action that is capable of being</p>\n<p>performed. In Creative Records vs. News GroupNews (TRL29.4.97, it was held that</p>\n<p>dramatic work must have movement, story or action and cannot be purely static.</p>\n<p>Types    of    rights.</p>\n<p>Moral Rights</p>\n<p>Moral Rights defined under S. 1 of the Copyright and NeighboringAct 2006 as means</p>\n<p>the right to claim ownership or authorship of performance as provided for under SS.10</p>\n<p>and 23 CRNRA.</p>\n<p>S.10 provides that</p>\n<p>“The author of any work protected by copyright law shall have amoral right to claim</p>\n<p>authorship of that work except where it included in current events by media /or other</p>\n<p>means to have theauthor’s name mentioned or acknowledged each time the work the</p>\n<p>work is used and to object and seek relief if there is any distortion, mutilation, alteration</p>\n<p>or modification of the work.</p>\n<p>The author can withdraw the work from circulation if it no longer reflects his conviction</p>\n<p>or intellectual concept and indemnify any authorized user affected by the withdrawal.</p>\n<p>Moral rights are not assignable except in cases of enforcement. They are categorized</p>\n<p>into two that is right of paternity and rightof integrity.</p>\n<p>Paternity</p>\n<p>This is the right of author to be made known to the public as creator of the work, i.e.</p>\n<p>mention my name if your using my work</p>\n<p>e.g. dissertations, writings etc.</p>\n<p>Integrity</p>\n<p>This is the right which prevents distortion of the work. The authorprevents his work</p>\n<p>from being destroyed.</p>\n<p>Moral rights exist independently of economic rights as they are concerned more with</p>\n<p>the work rather than commercial value only individuals have moral rights and not</p>\n<p>employees for example they cannot be assigned, disposed of by will or devolve by</p>\n<p>operation oflaw. Moral rights are not transferable are irrevocable.</p>\n<p>Moral rights are also provided for under Berne convention of 1979under Article. 6 and</p>\n<p>Article 5 of the WIPO Performance and Phonograms Treaty.</p>\n<p>Economic rights.</p>\n<p>Economic rights are provided for under S. 1 of the Copyright and Neighboring Act</p>\n<p>2006 as aright specified under S.9. It provides that;</p>\n<p>“The owner of the protected work shall have in relation to that work the exclusive right</p>\n<p>to do or authorize other persons to publish, produce and reproduce the work, distribute,</p>\n<p>perform thework in public, broadcast communicate it to the public commercially rent</p>\n<p>or sell original or copies of the work among others”.</p>\n<p>They are provided for under Article. 6 of the Berne convention of1979 and Articles 8</p>\n<p>and Article 9 of the WIPO Performance and Phonograms Treaty.</p>\n<p>These rights give the owner of a copyright the right to use his or her work for</p>\n<p>commercial purposes. They are transferable and canbe delegated unlike moral rights.</p>\n<p>Rights that bring in money.eg sale, reproduce.</p>\n<p>IP is money, just look at your self-everything is IP dress, hair style design etc. Economic</p>\n<p>rights are transferable e.g. when I write a book and give it to another. You go away</p>\n<p>without claimingany rights any more, you remain with moral rights.</p>\n<h2 id=\"eligibility-requirements-for-protection\">ELIGIBILITY/ REQUIREMENTS FOR PROTECTION.</h2>\n<p>The laws of the country provide for the subject matter of protection as well as</p>\n<p>eligibility requirement. Gambian copyrightact provides work shall not be eligible for</p>\n<h4 id=\"copyright-protection-unless\">copyright protection unless:</h4>\n<p>i)     its original in character</p>\n<p>ii)    It has been affixed in a defined medium</p>\n<p>iii)   Created by citizens.</p>\n<p>Nevertheless, Copyright Law does not operate in a vacuum. In fact, for a work to be</p>\n<h4 id=\"considered-copyrightable-it-must-evidently-conform-to-four-basic-principles\">considered copyrightable it must evidently conform to four basic principles:</h4>\n<p>iv)    (a) originality</p>\n<p>v)     (b) non- application to ideas</p>\n<p>vi)    (c) material form and</p>\n<p>vii) (d) principle de minims non curat lex.</p>\n<p>Originality</p>\n<p>This requirement is to give an author an exclusive right of exploitation.</p>\n<p>In relation to the requirement for originality, the term original does not have its ordinary</p>\n<p>dictionary meaning. Originality here is more concerned with the manner in which the</p>\n<p>work was created and the work need not be unique or meritorious. The true test is that</p>\n<p>the work must have originated from the author. In Ladbroke(Football) Ltd V William</p>\n<p>Hill (Football) Ltd [1964]1 WLR 273 at 291</p>\n<p>Held: it was held that the word original only requires that the work should not be</p>\n<p>copied but should originate from the author. This definition allows for a wider scope</p>\n<p>of original works. For as long as the author labored to create the work as embodied in</p>\n<p>the ‘Sweat of the brow doctrine’ but not only that, that such an author involved</p>\n<p>sufficient amount of skill, labor and judgment (SSLJ) in the creation of the work, then</p>\n<p>his work can be said to be original.</p>\n<p>Only original work is protected. There is no requirement of novelty and non-</p>\n<p>obviousness as with patents, but the work must have originated from the author. The</p>\n<p>work must involve a sufficient amount of skill, labor and judgment (SSLL).</p>\n<p>Unlike the novelty requirement under patent law, the concept of originality in copyright</p>\n<p>law is modest. Focus is on something one</p>\n<p>produces as an author. Sweat of the brow alone is not enough. There has to be a creative</p>\n<p>spark.</p>\n<p>In Feist Publications Inc. rural Telephone Services co, court held, among other things,</p>\n<p>“Original, as the term is used in copyright, means only that the work was indecently</p>\n<p>created by the author (as opposed to copied from other works), and that it possesses at</p>\n<p>least some minimal degree of creativity….. the vast majority of works make the grade</p>\n<p>quite easily, as they possess some spark ofcreativity:, there has to be some contribution</p>\n<p>by the author.</p>\n<p>The challenge for copyright law is to reward originality and creativity, but not at the</p>\n<p>expense of protecting purely factual material and inhibiting the development of further</p>\n<p>technological inventions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-4-of-the-copyright-and-neighboring-act-2006-provides-that\">Section 4 of the Copyright and Neighboring Act 2006 provides that;</h3>\n<p>“work shall not be eligible for protection unless it’s original and reduced t material</p>\n<p>form”.</p>\n<p>The work is original if it the product of the independent effort of the author. This</p>\n<p>requirement is to give an author an exclusive right to exploit his work. Work for</p>\n<p>protection must have originatedfrom the author and does not require originality per se</p>\n<p>or inventive thought but should be the work involves sufficient amount of effort,</p>\n<p>Judgment and skill. Work need only to beshown to have originated from the</p>\n<p>author it does not require original or inventive thought but only that the work should</p>\n<p>not be</p>\n<p>copied and that the author should have expanded a sufficient amount of labor,</p>\n<p>Judgment, skill and effort.</p>\n<p>The originality that is required for copyright purposes relates to the expression and not</p>\n<p>the idea embodied therein. It must involvesome creativity. Dicks vs Brookes;</p>\n<p>“court held that ………….so long as someone has invested sufficient skill and</p>\n<p>labor to give the resultant work a new character is termed original”.</p>\n<p>Creation Records Limited vs News Group Newspaper Ltd. Lord Atkinson</p>\n<p>said,</p>\n<p>“it is the product of la labor, skill and capital of one man</p>\n<p>which must not be appropriated by another not the elements, the raw materials</p>\n<p>of one may use expression upon which the first have been expanded. To secure</p>\n<p>copyright this product it is necessary that labor, skill and capital should be</p>\n<p>expanded sufficiently….”.</p>\n<p>The standard of originality is a question of fact or degree. Everycase would depend</p>\n<p>on its particulars.</p>\n<p>Consider the case of Hajji Ntege Ssebagala vs MTN and another in detail for this</p>\n<p>application in the Ugandan case.</p>\n<p>Emerson vs Davies.</p>\n<p>Simplicity</p>\n<p>simplicity    is not a reason           to deny        copyright.         Expression</p>\n<p>newspaper vs, Liverpool daily post and… ltd. Grid and sequence</p>\n<p>of letters business letters as held in British water co ltd vs liquid air ltd. Calendars,</p>\n<p>categories, contract forms directorates to mention but a few.</p>\n<p>Translations</p>\n<p>Translations of literary works critical annotations and explanationshave been held to be</p>\n<p>eligible for copyright protection. This was illustrated in the case of Byrne vs statist co.</p>\n<p>(1914) 1KB 622 andin Cummins vs Bond (1927) 1 Ch 167</p>\n<p>Compilation</p>\n<p>Compilation is essentially collection of pre-existing materials. It draws from a body of</p>\n<p>materials some of which might have existedin the public domain and for that reason</p>\n<p>the originality is satisfiedby the fact of selection, arrangements organization of those</p>\n<p>materials contained in the compilation. In determining compilation courts have looked</p>\n<p>out for labor, skill evidence. The arguments upheld are though not clear cut arguments</p>\n<p>of broadcasting programs receipt books, directory etc.</p>\n<p>Cramps vs. Southern Information; the issue was,</p>\n<p>whether the label of can be described as original literary artistic works and it was held</p>\n<p>by HOL that; was the opinion that the bundle of information furnished in the</p>\n<p>respondent’s dairy was common place thus the selection in the case did not constitute</p>\n<p>anoriginal literary works.</p>\n<p>According to ECA E’ ano products vs. Man dopes held that thedefendant is at liberty</p>\n<p>to make use of any information which was</p>\n<p>in public domain but was not entitled to copy the plaintiff’scompilation there by making</p>\n<p>use of the letters skill and judgment and saving himself the trouble and cost of</p>\n<p>assembling his own.</p>\n<p>b) Material form/ Fixation.</p>\n<p>S.4 (1) states that the author of any work specified in section 5 shall have a right of</p>\n<p>protection of the work, where work is original and is reduced to material form in</p>\n<p>whatever method irrespective of quality of the work or the purpose for which it is</p>\n<p>created.</p>\n<p>Copyright only accrues where the work is original and is reduced to material form in</p>\n<p>any method and it doesn’t matter whether thework is of poor quality or for whatever</p>\n<p>purpose. However, this is not meant to subject the author to formalities in order to</p>\n<p>afford protection but aims at aiding the process of ascertaining works.</p>\n<p>S.4 (2) states that the protection of the author’s work shall not besubject to any formally.</p>\n<p>According to 4(3), a work is original if it isthe product of the independent efforts of the</p>\n<p>author.</p>\n<p>However, copyright law in Uganda does not protect ideas, concepts, and procedures</p>\n<p>inter alia. This is in tandem with the Trade Related Aspects of Intellectual Property</p>\n<p>Rights (TRIPS)Agreement, which also provides that copyright protection shall</p>\n<p>extend to expressions and not to ideas, method of operation or mathematical concepts.</p>\n<p>Idea and expression of idea dichotomy</p>\n<p>Article 9 92) of the TRIPS Agreement provides that copyright protection shall extend</p>\n<p>to expressions and not to ideas, procedures, and methods of operation or mathematical</p>\n<p>concepts. As such; copyright law protects only the form of expression of ideas not the</p>\n<p>ideas themselves. i.e. ideas, concepts, procedures, methods or other things of a similar</p>\n<p>nature are to be protected (See sec. 6 of the Act). The creativity protected by copyright</p>\n<p>lawsis creativity in the choice and the arrangement of words, musical notes, colors,</p>\n<p>shapes etc. legality and quality are irrelevant. (Notethat the requirement of quality under</p>\n<p>civil law is strong e.g. the work must bear the author’s personality i.e. a creative effort).</p>\n<p>This was the same view. In Donoghue v. Allied Newspapers [1938] 1ch 106 at 109</p>\n<p>“A person may have a brilliant idea for a story, or a picture or a play, and one which</p>\n<p>appears to him to be original; if he communicates that idea to an author or an artist or</p>\n<p>a playwright, the production which is the result of the communication of the idea to</p>\n<p>the author or the artist or the playwright is the copyright of the person who has</p>\n<p>clothed the idea in form, whether by</p>\n<p>means of a picture, a play, or a book, and the owner of the idea has no rights in that</p>\n<p>product.”</p>\n<p>The benefit of this is to avoid immaterial claims that would in many cases be difficult</p>\n<p>to assess and also to encourage thosethat conceive ideas to go ahead and express</p>\n<p>them. In the same light, to promote creative works by the fact of recognizing only works</p>\n<p>that have gone past the stage of ideas and have been expressed in conspicuous ways.</p>\n<p>Fixation is required because it helps to show what is the work and helps in showing</p>\n<p>paintings and permanence.</p>\n<p>In Plix Products Ltd Vs Frank (1985) 5 IPR 156, court stated that there is a distinction</p>\n<p>between the general idea or basic concept ofa work and the details of form and shape</p>\n<p>which are used to transform the basic concept into concrete form. When the ideas</p>\n<p>which are essentially constructive in character are reduced to concrete form, the form</p>\n<p>they take is where copyright lies.</p>\n<p>C) Principle de minimis non curat lex the law does not concern itself with trifles)</p>\n<p>In ascertaining whether a work is significant enough to afford copyright. In Sinanide v</p>\n<p>la maisonkosmeo (1928) 139 LT 365, Held: it was held that to quote a bit of a sentence</p>\n<p>of a literally was too small a matter on which to base a copyright infringement</p>\n<p>action. Basically for one to sustain an action in copyright infringement, a substantial</p>\n<h4 id=\"part-of-the-work-must-be-copied-to-include\">part of the work must be copied to include:</h4>\n<p>I.   Exact usage of part of the work, taken verbatim or</p>\n<p>II.   Some reworking of the whole of it or</p>\n<p>III.   A combination of these.</p>\n<p>This aims at reducing frivolous claims and not opening the floodgates which situation</p>\n<p>would overwhelm the courts.</p>\n<h2 id=\"registration-requirement\">REGISTRATION REQUIREMENT</h2>\n<p>There are no registration requirements for protection under copyright law, with an</p>\n<p>exception of the US. The said protection lasts for the author’s life plus 50 years. Upon</p>\n<p>expiration, the workenters the public domain.</p>\n<p>Article 5 of the Berne Convention (BC) provides that “the enjoyment and the exercise</p>\n<p>of these rights shall not be subject toany formality.”</p>\n<p>Under US law [Sec.411 (a) of the 1976 Copyright Act registration was a mandatory</p>\n<p>requirement contrary to Art.5 (BC). This position was subsequently altered so as to</p>\n<p>strike a compromise in1988. Sec.411(a) as amended made optional in the case offoreign</p>\n<p>works claiming copyright under the Convention. Notwithstanding, the requirement of</p>\n<p>registration remains a statutory perquisite for US copyright owners.</p>\n<p>It is argued that registration provides additional benefits with respect to the recovery of</p>\n<p>damages and attorneys’ fees. There are further policy arguments for retaining</p>\n<h4 id=\"registration\">registration:</h4>\n<p>• Complete public record of protected works</p>\n<p>• Registration simplifies and expedites litigationsSEE CNRA</p>\n<h2 id=\"authorship-and-ownership\">AUTHORSHIP AND OWNERSHIP</h2>\n<p>Authorship</p>\n<p>The question of who is an author also depends on the facts andcircumstances without</p>\n<p>losing sight of the definition.”</p>\n<p>Author</p>\n<h4 id=\"the-osborn-x27-s-concise-law-dictionary1-also-defines-author-as\">The Osborn&#x27;s Concise Law Dictionary1 also defines “author” as:</h4>\n<p>&quot;…….. the author is the person who creates the work.This is taken to be</p>\n<p>the producer of the sound recording, the producer and principal director</p>\n<p>of the film, the person making the broadcast, the publisher inthe case of</p>\n<p>the typographical arrangement of a published edition.&quot;</p>\n<p>In UK, the Copyright Act 1956 section 35 (5) thereof whichdefines the word &quot;author&quot;</p>\n<p>in relation to sound recording or a cinematographic film to mean the maker of the</p>\n<p>recording or the film.</p>\n<p>The definition of the word “author” under section 2 of the Copyright and</p>\n<p>Neighbouring Rights Act is not exhaustive and requires other provision for a definite</p>\n<p>meaning. section 4 (1) of the Copyright and Neighbouring Rights Act, protects the</p>\n<p>works of an author as listed under section 5 of the Act. In other words, anauthor is</p>\n<p>associated with &quot;works&quot; and the creation of such works or work.</p>\n<p>It is vital to identify the author of the work due to the following reasons: -</p>\n<p>i)     The     validity   of    copy    right     is    based       on       the   author’s</p>\n<p>citizenship/domicile.</p>\n<p>ii)    This could help in determination of persons who is entitled to the 1st</p>\n<p>ownership of the copyright in question</p>\n<p>iii)   To determine          whether      there        has   been        a    transfer    or</p>\n<p>transmission of a subsisting copyright.</p>\n<p>iv)    The term literary, artistic or musical work is generally</p>\n<p>defined with reference the life of the author of the work. it</p>\n<p>is therefore, imperative in such cases that the author of the work must first</p>\n<p>be ascertained so as to be able to ascertain whether he is living or dead and</p>\n<p>come to a conclusion on whether the copyright term is subsisting or expired</p>\n<p>already.</p>\n<p>v)     Moral rights are conferred on the author irrespective of the transfer or</p>\n<p>separate existence of economic rights.</p>\n<p>The author is the person who creates the work or who made the production of the</p>\n<p>work possible.</p>\n<p>Nonetheless, David Bainbridge expounded the expression “authorship” He made an</p>\n<p>important distinction between an authorand the owner. Which distinction the CNRA</p>\n<p>does not make.</p>\n<p>Owner</p>\n<p>A person o whom the right to exploit the copyright control vests. The owner is not</p>\n<p>necessarily the author of the work.</p>\n<p>He expresses the view that the author of the work has moral rights while the owner of</p>\n<p>the works has economic rights. These two rights may be merged in one person but that</p>\n<p>is not always necessarily the case. Sometimes the author of the work will also be the</p>\n<p>owner of the copyright in the work.</p>\n<p>Ownership flows from authorship, which means the person who makes the work is</p>\n<p>normally the first owner of the copyright in thework, provided he did not create</p>\n<p>the work in the course ofemployment.</p>\n<p>Authorship is a question of law and fact. Authorship is not negotiated parties cannot</p>\n<p>shift the fact of Authorship by agreement. In the case of Beggars Banquet Records vs.</p>\n<p>CartoonTelevision and Another.it was held that it should not be inter pretend to stop</p>\n<p>the second defendant from contending that anyone other than the plaintiff had made</p>\n<p>the agreement.</p>\n<p>As far as they use of devices is concerned as in the case of soundrecordings the “author”</p>\n<p>is deemed to be the person by whom the arrangements necessary for the creation of</p>\n<p>the work was undertaken. The author of a sound recording is the producer according</p>\n<p>to the statutory law. Under the Ugandan law section 2 of the CNRA, 2006 defines a</p>\n<p>producer to mean the person who organizes and finances the production of an</p>\n<p>audiovisual fixationor sound recording.</p>\n<p>There were suggestions of materials for the creation of the work</p>\n<p>does not make the person offering the suggestions an author. In the case of Tate vs.</p>\n<p>Thomas (1921)1 Ch 503 it was held that; the</p>\n<p>defendants had produced the first version of the dramatic work inwhich the plaintiff</p>\n<p>claimed copyright. The defendant relied on the claims of one Peterman who based his</p>\n<p>claim to the work of his general involvement which indeed suggesting the title.</p>\n<p>The court declined to accept Peterman’s contributions as sufficient to sustain the claim</p>\n<p>to partake in the copyright and heldthat he was neither the sole author nor a joint author.</p>\n<p>In Walter vs Lane (1900) AC 539 House of Lords held that, thenews reporter did have</p>\n<p>copyright since there was no requirement of originality in thought or language by them.</p>\n<p>Moreover, where work is created by more than one person and noparticular part of the</p>\n<p>work is identified to have      been made by each person, such that the work is</p>\n<p>indistinguishable, all theauthors shall be co-owners of the economic rights and moral</p>\n<p>rights relating to that work and the co-owners shall have equal rights in that work.</p>\n<h4 id=\"in-essence-this-is-co-authorship-joint-co-authorship\">In essence this is co-authorship/ Joint co-authorship:</h4>\n<p>Consider the case of Angella Katatumba and another Vs ACODE and another.</p>\n<p>A simple test for determining authorship in cases of more than one person where he</p>\n<p>has made contribution to the creation of thework is to identify the resultant work and</p>\n<p>ask who was responsible for providing the necessary skill and labor. Where onehad an</p>\n<p>idea and clothed by another in his words or other form of expression the copyright will</p>\n<p>subsist in the person who has clothed the idea so received in the protectable term.</p>\n<h4 id=\"example\">Example:</h4>\n<p>If a man employs a service of a ghost writer to write his biography based on materials</p>\n<p>supplied by the subject, the copyright in the final product would accordingly belong to</p>\n<p>the ghost writer. In contrast in the case of Donoghue vs. Allied Newspaper Ltd</p>\n<p>(1938)1 Ch106 at 109 Farwell J said a</p>\n<p>“person may have a brilliant idea for a story or a picture or for aplay and of which appear</p>\n<p>to him to be original, if he communicates that idea to an author or an artist or a play</p>\n<p>writer,the production which is the results of the communication of theidea to author</p>\n<p>or an artist or a play writer, is the copyright of theperson who in this case the author</p>\n<p>clothed the idea in form, thenthe copyright will go to him who clothed the idea…”.</p>\n<p>Employment</p>\n<p>Copyright in Journalistic Works</p>\n<p>Works made by journalists who are employed under a contract of service belong to the</p>\n<p>employer. The journalist has the right to photocopy the written work and include it in</p>\n<p>a book. For freelanceand independent journalists, in the absence of an agreement, the</p>\n<p>journalist owns the copyright.</p>\n<p>Copy right in Commissioned works</p>\n<p>Nevertheless, the actual author forfeits copyright in the case of works done for</p>\n<p>commission, under employment, for government or international bodies as the case</p>\n<p>may be. However, this positioncan be altered by an agreement to the contrary. In Prof.</p>\n<p>George</p>\n<p>W. Kakoma vs. Attorney General, H.C.C Suit No. 197 Of 2008 Before: The</p>\n<p>Honourable Mr. Justice Yorokamu Bamwine defined Copyright as a ‘set of exclusive</p>\n<p>rights granted to the author or creator of an original work, including the right to copy,</p>\n<p>distribute and adopt the work. These rights can be licensed, transferred and/or</p>\n<p>assigned. Copyright lasts for a certain time period after which the work is said to enter</p>\n<p>the public domain.’</p>\n<h4 id=\"court-in-this-case-was-of-the-view-that\">Court in this case was of the view that:</h4>\n<p>“As a general rule, copyright in a commissioned work belongs to the author, in the</p>\n<p>absence of an express or implied term to the contrary. Where the contract is unclear as</p>\n<p>to the rights of the Commissioner to use the work, the courts may imply the grant of</p>\n<p>an appropriate right. And if the lacuna can be satisfied by the grant of a license rather</p>\n<p>than an assignment of the copyright,then the court will imply the former.”</p>\n<p>The commissioner – ownership rule is subject to two qualifications which need to be</p>\n<p>addressed before:-</p>\n<p>i)     The commissioned agreements must be made for valuableconsideration.</p>\n<p>ii)    Where the purpose of commissioning is expressly or by implication made</p>\n<p>known to the author at the time ofagreement, the author can restrain its use</p>\n<p>for any extraneous purpose.</p>\n<p>Court further noted that certain transactions are, valid only if effected by deed. For</p>\n<p>example, under common law and also the CNRA 2006, for the transaction to be</p>\n<p>effective in the case of an assignment of copyright or exclusive license, it must be in</p>\n<p>writing and signed by or on behalf of the present copyright owner. The</p>\n<p>law is couched in mandatory terms. However, failure to execute aformal assignment</p>\n<p>would not ipso facto vitiate the contract.</p>\n<p>Also consider the case of Nakide vs Bank of Uganda for the discussions of</p>\n<p>commissioned work.</p>\n<p>Infringement of a copyright &amp; related rights and remedies</p>\n<p>S.46. of the Act infringement of a copyright occurs when person without a valid</p>\n<p>transfer, license, assignment or other authorizationunder the act deals with the work or</p>\n<p>does any act falling within the exclusive rights of a copyright owner. Infringement</p>\n<p>includes use of work in a manner prejudicial to the honor and reputationof the</p>\n<p>authors. S.46(2). Infringements where defendants without authority from the owner,</p>\n<p>copies or uses the copyrighted materials.</p>\n<p>Indirect infringement occurs when the defendants authorizes a third party to use</p>\n<p>copyrighted material without any authority todo so.</p>\n<p>See: university of new south Wales vs. Moore house. The issuethe court considered was</p>\n<p>whether the university had authored infringement was held inter alia that the university</p>\n<p>authorized theact of Brenna in making the copies in question which wereinfringing</p>\n<p>copies.</p>\n<p>The substantial similarity test for infringement</p>\n<p>The question of substantiality often arises, i.e. exactly what amount of work should be</p>\n<p>copied</p>\n<p>)</p>\n<p>• The first thing to look at is the part taken in comparison tothe copyrighted</p>\n<p>material</p>\n<p>• The course use a qualitative assessment not a quantitativeone</p>\n<p>• In the digital era, providers are seen as infringers, but as vehicles for</p>\n<p>infringement</p>\n<p>See: MP3.com v. universal the court held that mp3.com willfullyinfringed copyright</p>\n<p>owned by universal music when it assembleda data base of CDs and allowed its</p>\n<p>customers to download music from the internet</p>\n<p>In Francis day &amp; hunter ltd Vs bron, it was held that if the works are sufficiently/</p>\n<p>objectively similar, this may raise a presumption of copying. There is need for a casual</p>\n<p>connection between the original and the infringing items otherwise coincidental</p>\n<p>copying is not sufficient to Constitute copying and infringements extendedto partial</p>\n<p>copying in relation to substantive part.</p>\n<p>Francis Day &amp; Hunter v. Bron [1963] 1 Ch 587.</p>\n<p>“There must be present two elements: first there must be presentsufficient objective</p>\n<p>similarity between the infringing work and the copyright work, or a substantial part</p>\n<p>thereof, for the former to beproperty described, not necessarily identical with but as a</p>\n<p>reproduction or adaptation of the latter; secondly, the copyright work must be the</p>\n<p>source from which the infringing work isderived.”</p>\n<p>See also; Designers Guild Ltd. v. Russell Williams (Textiles) Ltd.</p>\n<p>[2001] 1 All E.R. 700.</p>\n<p>Indirect copying</p>\n<p>In Plix products vs. Frank, the issue of indirect copying was considered. It constituted</p>\n<p>a verbal description of the plaintiff’s designs by the defendants. It was held that the</p>\n<p>verbal descriptionof a copyrighted work may be a medium for indirect copying subject</p>\n<p>to certain criteria unless it is an independent creation. It was further stated that there is</p>\n<p>indirect copying of the plaintiffs copyright if there is a marked degree of similarity and</p>\n<p>deriving theparts from literary description with sufficient casual connection.</p>\n<p>In auto desk Vs Dyson. It was held that a person who reproducesa set of instructions</p>\n<p>by turning the source code into an object code does not escape liability or infringement.</p>\n<p>Indirect copying</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-it-is-essential-that-an-author-s-copyright-is-not-only-protected-from-direct\">1. It is essential that an author’s copyright is not only protected from direct</h3>\n<p>infringement but also from indirect infringement.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-the-enforcement-of-rights-against-a-person-who-authorizes-the-infringement-of\">2. The enforcement of rights against a person who authorizes the infringement of</h3>\n<p>copyright acts as a deterrent not only to copyright infringers but also to</p>\n<p>associated people.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-in-light-of-the-information-technology-revolution-and-the-implementation-of-the\">3. In light of the information technology revolution, and the implementation of the</h3>\n<p>internet, the scope of copyright infringement has been exponentially increased.</p>\n<h3 id=\"4-the-rate-at-which-dissemination-of-information-albeit-protected-by-copyright\">4. The rate at which dissemination of information, albeit protected by copyright,</h3>\n<p>has increased due to internet highlights the increased need to provide greater</p>\n<p>security for the authors.</p>\n<h3 id=\"5-the-growth-of-the-internet-has-seen-the-rise-of-the-hacker-mentality-and\">5. The growth of the internet has seen the rise of the hacker mentality and</h3>\n<p>entitlement philosophy; majority of internet users tend to think they have right</p>\n<p>to information for free, and more particularly the music.</p>\n<h3 id=\"6-see-signing-reproduction-without-authority\">6. See signing –Reproduction without authority.</h3>\n<p>Limitations on Rights</p>\n<p>Strictly speaking, the first limitation is the exclusion from copyright protection of</p>\n<p>certain categories of works. In somecountries, as you have heard, works are excluded</p>\n<p>from protection if they are not fixed in tangible form; for example, a work of</p>\n<p>choreography would only be protected once the movements werewritten down in dance</p>\n<p>notation or recorded on videotape. In some (but not all) countries, moreover, the texts</p>\n<p>of laws, court and administrative decisions are excluded from copyright protection.</p>\n<p>The second category of limitations on the rights of authors and other owners of</p>\n<p>copyright concerns particular acts of exploitation,normally requiring the authorization</p>\n<p>of the owner of rights, which may, under circumstances specified in the law, be done</p>\n<p>without authorization. There are two basic types of limitations in this category:</p>\n<p>1) Free uses, which are acts of exploitation of works that may be carried out without</p>\n<p>authorization and without an obligation to compensate the owner of rights for</p>\n<p>the use.</p>\n<p>2) Non-voluntary licenses, under which the acts of exploitation may be carried out</p>\n<p>without authorization, but with the obligation to compensate the owner of rights.</p>\n<p>Examples of free uses include: the making of quotations from aprotected work,</p>\n<p>provided that the source of the quotation, including the name of the author, is</p>\n<p>mentioned and that the extent of the quotation is compatible with fair practice; use</p>\n<p>of works by way of illustration for teaching purposes; and use of works for the</p>\n<p>purpose of news reporting. In respect of the right of reproduction, the Berne</p>\n<h4 id=\"convention-contains-a-general-rule-rather-than-explicit-detailed-limitations\">Convention contains a general rule, rather than explicit detailed limitations:</h4>\n<p>Article 9[2] provides that member States may provide for free reproduction in</p>\n<p>“certain special cases” where the acts do not conflict with a normal exploitation of</p>\n<p>the work and do not unreasonably prejudice the legitimate interests of the author.</p>\n<p>As noted above, numerous laws contain provisions allowing reproduction of a work</p>\n<p>exclusively for the personal, private andnon-commercial use of individuals.</p>\n<p>However, the ease and quality of this individual copying, made possible by</p>\n<p>audiotaping or videotaping and even more recent technological improvements, has</p>\n<p>led some countries to narrow the scope of such provisions. Certain legal systems</p>\n<p>allow copying but incorporate a mechanism for payment to owners ofrights for the</p>\n<p>prejudice to their economic interests, though afee imposed on sales of blank tapes</p>\n<p>and / or tape recorders.</p>\n<p>In addition to specific free uses enumerated in national laws, the laws of some</p>\n<p>countries recognize the concept known as fairuse or fair dealing, which allows use</p>\n<p>of works without the authorization of the owner of rights, taking into account</p>\n<p>factorssuch as the following: the nature and purpose of the use, including whether</p>\n<p>it is for commercial purposes; the nature of the work used; the amount of the</p>\n<p>work used in relation towork as a whole; and the likely effect of the use</p>\n<p>on thepotential commercial value of the work.</p>\n<p>As noted above, non-voluntary licenses allow use of works in certain circumstances</p>\n<p>without the authorization of the owner ofrights, but which, by operation of law,</p>\n<p>require that compensation be paid in respect of the use. Such licenses are called</p>\n<p>“non-voluntary” because they are authorized by the law and do not result from the</p>\n<p>exercise of the copyright owner’s exclusive right to authorize particular acts. Non-</p>\n<p>voluntary licenses were usually created in circumstances where a new technology for</p>\n<p>the dissemination of works to the public had emerged, and where the national</p>\n<p>legislature feared that owners of rights would prevent the development of the new</p>\n<p>technology by refusing to authorize use of works. This was true in the Berne</p>\n<p>Convention, which recognized two forms of non- voluntary licenses: firstly, to allow</p>\n<p>the mechanical reproduction of musical works and secondly for broadcasting. It</p>\n<p>should be noted, however, that the justification for no-voluntary licenses is called</p>\n<p>increasingly into question, since effective alternatives now exist for making works</p>\n<p>available to the public based on authorizations given by the owners of rights,</p>\n<p>including in the</p>\n<p>form of collective administration of rights. Whatever the situation in your country</p>\n<p>relating to copyright there will inevitably be situations occurring where copyright</p>\n<p>is infringedso it is important to consider the types of remedies the copyright owner</p>\n<p>can take.</p>\n<p>Conclusion</p>\n<p>• It is essential that an author’s copyright is not only protected from direct</p>\n<p>infringement but also from indirect infringement.</p>\n<p>• The enforcement of rights against a person who authorizesthe infringement</p>\n<p>of copyright acts as a deterrent not only to copyright infringers but also to</p>\n<p>associated people.</p>\n<p>• In light of the information technology revolution, and the implementation of</p>\n<p>the internet the scope of copyright infringement has been exponentially</p>\n<p>increased.</p>\n<p>• The rate at which dissemination of Information, albeit protected by</p>\n<p>copyright. They increased due to internet</p>\n<p>highlights the increased need to provide greater security for the rights of</p>\n<p>authors.</p>\n<p>• The growth of the internet has seen the rise of the hacker</p>\n<p>mentality and entitlement philosophy, majority of internet users tend to think</p>\n<p>they have right to information for free and more particularly the music.</p>\n<p>• See singing- reproduction without authority.</p>\n<p>Right to perform</p>\n<p>Under S.5 (1) (b) a copyright in a musical work shall be the exclusive right to control</p>\n<p>the public performance for payment. Other persons other than the owners and agents</p>\n<p>who wish to perform must have the consent or permission of the authorotherwise it</p>\n<p>constitutes infringement. Under S.11, infringement occurs when a person does any act</p>\n<p>causing falling within the copyright without the license of the owner</p>\n<p>Defenses to a copyright actions.</p>\n<p>A defendant who is sued for infringement of copyright may plead the defense of fair</p>\n<p>dealing. This provided for under sec.15 of the Act sec.9 and 46.</p>\n<p>FAIR USE PRIVILEGES 5(2)(a)</p>\n<p>Fair use or fair dealing (US equivalent) is one of the statutory defences available for</p>\n<p>defendants who would otherwise be liable for infringement. It arises in the minimize</p>\n<p>use of non-substantial parts, research and study, backup copies of computer programs</p>\n<p>etc. Its objective is to strike balance between private incentives and social gains.</p>\n<p>There is always a temptation by the defendant to say that he did not make a profit. The</p>\n<p>court is not interested in that, but rather inthe fact that the copyright is not infringed.</p>\n<p>The other defences available are where the work is for the purpose of criticism or</p>\n<p>review, reporting news or giving professional advice. The first two defences are</p>\n<p>also available</p>\n<p>where fair dealing is made of an AV item- a sound recording, cinematograph film,</p>\n<p>sound broadcast or TV broadcast.</p>\n<p>All use of copyright material is free and without formality. For purposes of research</p>\n<p>and study however, copying must be made in reasonable portions. In De Garis v.</p>\n<p>Neville Jeffress Pidler Pty Ltd, the court held that the provision of press clippings for</p>\n<p>a fee was not “research”</p>\n<p>“Research” means a “diligent and systematic inquiry or investigation into a subject in</p>\n<p>order to discover facts or principles.”</p>\n<p>“Study” means “application of the mind to the acquisition ofknowledge as by</p>\n<p>reading, investigation or reflection.”</p>\n<p>Factors that are used to determine fairness are;</p>\n<p>• Purpose and character of the use</p>\n<p>• Nature of the work or adaptation</p>\n<p>• Possibility    of obtaining       the work or adaptation                within    a</p>\n<p>reasonable time at an ordinary commercial price</p>\n<p>• Effect of the use upon the potential market for or value ofthe work or</p>\n<p>adaptation; and</p>\n<p>• Amount         and substantiality        of   the    part   copied     taken     in</p>\n<p>relation to the whole or adaptation.</p>\n<p>For the success of the defence of fair use, sufficient acknowledgement of the work</p>\n<p>must be made. “….an acknowledgement identifying the work by its title or other</p>\n<p>description and, unless the work is anonymous or pseudonymous</p>\n<p>or the author has previously agreed or directed that an acknowledgement of his or her</p>\n<p>name is not to be made, also identifying the author”.</p>\n<p>See Express Newspaper plc v News(uk) Ltd where the court held that a cursory</p>\n<p>reference to the fact that the quotations came from another newspaper was not</p>\n<p>sufficient acknowledgement.</p>\n<p>Principles involving the defence of fair dealing</p>\n<p>Fair dealing involves questions of degree and impression; it is to be judged by the</p>\n<p>criterion of a fair minded and honest person andis an abstract concept;</p>\n<p>Fairness is to be judged objectively in relation to the relevant purpose i.e. the purpose</p>\n<p>of criterion or review or the purpose of reporting;</p>\n<h4 id=\"criticism-and-review-are\">Criticism and review are:</h4>\n<p>• Words       of wide       and     indefinite    scope      which     should     be</p>\n<p>interpreted liberally</p>\n<p>• May strongly be expressed</p>\n<p>• Must be genuine, need not necessarily be balanced</p>\n<p>• Must not involve an oblique or hidden motive particularlywhere the trade</p>\n<p>rivalry is involved.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-public-interest\">2. Public interest</h3>\n<p>Public policy may dictate the certain work should not qualify for protection i.e. works</p>\n<p>that are immoral or constitutes criminaloffences.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-sec-14-assignment-and-license\">3. Sec 14 assignment and license</h3>\n<p>The appropriate forum an action for copyright infringement is the high court</p>\n<p>commercial division</p>\n<p>In terms of enforcement of copyright, the act provides creation ofcollecting societies</p>\n<p>which are companies ltd by guarantees</p>\n<h2 id=\"b-related-rights\">B) RELATED RIGHTS</h2>\n<p>Copyright is no longer the domain of authors and artists. This is because a field of rights</p>\n<p>related to copyright has developedrapidly, called “related rights” (also known as allied</p>\n<p>rights or neighboring rights).</p>\n<p>Related rights enjoy similar protection to that of copyrights, albeitlimited and for a</p>\n<p>shorter duration.</p>\n<p>Assignment &amp; Licensing</p>\n<p>To assign is to transfer proprietary rights.</p>\n<p>To license is to grant permission to someone to make use of yourworks.</p>\n<p>Unless commercialized i.e. transform IP into services, IP is notuseful.</p>\n<p>A number of questions arise as to who is entitled to assign ortransfer IPR’s is joint</p>\n<p>ownership.</p>\n<p>Joint ownership and joint authorship are different. A work of jointauthorship is</p>\n<p>produced          by the collaboration          of two or more</p>\n<p>people. Contributions of each party must not be separable from the other’s</p>\n<p>contributions.</p>\n<p>For joint authors’ rights to be established, agreements are cardinal.</p>\n<p>Joint ownership can rise by authorship, contract or inheritance. Joint owners are</p>\n<p>therefore not permitted to exercise their rights independent of the other. Unless</p>\n<p>contracted our joint ownership isforever.</p>\n<p>Assignment</p>\n<p>Once an assignment takes effect, all rights vest in the assignee. The assignee has the</p>\n<p>right to alter the work, provided moralrights are not fringed.</p>\n<p>Formalities for Assignment</p>\n<p>Must be in writing (in some instances, oral agreements to transferwill be effective in</p>\n<p>equity)</p>\n<p>An assignment for future copyright is possible but must be inwriting.</p>\n<p>See; Chaplain v Leslie Frewin (Publishers) Ltd [1966] 1 Ch. 71(CA)</p>\n<p>An assignment was found in a contract in which a clause read“the publisher shall</p>\n<p>during the legal term of the copyright have the exclusive right of producing, publishing</p>\n<p>and selling the said works…. “Held, this was sufficient to assign all future works.</p>\n<p>Licensing</p>\n<p>A license    is a contract        and     is a fundamental        mechanism        for</p>\n<p>commercializing IPR’s.</p>\n<p>There are three (3) types of licenses, sole, exclusive and non-exclusive.</p>\n<p>With licenses, ownership does not pass.</p>\n<p>Under English law, a license granted by a copyright owner is binding on every successor</p>\n<p>in title.</p>\n<p>Licenses are usually personal to the owner and are therefore not assignable.</p>\n<p>Alterations to the license depend on the terms of the license and the author’s moral</p>\n<p>rights not to have his work subject to derogatory treatment.</p>\n<h2 id=\"digital-challenge-to-copyright\">DIGITAL CHALLENGE TO COPYRIGHT</h2>\n<p>Copyright laws were designed in the analogue era, and as such, the digital world has</p>\n<p>posed enormous challenges because digital work has a remarkable breadth of</p>\n<p>descriptive ability, including text, audio, video, software and shapes. Traditional</p>\n<p>broadcasting disseminated information from one source, but a new medium has been</p>\n<p>introduced by digital technology i.e. convergence of broadcasting and</p>\n<p>telecommunications, both on the same network, interactive broadcasting and the</p>\n<p>internet.</p>\n<p>Issues Brought By Digitisation</p>\n<p>Internet has resulted in ease of copying, manipulation and storage of works, which</p>\n<p>can be disseminated worldwide in a</p>\n<p>matter of seconds. Copyright owners have consequently been lobbying for an overhaul</p>\n<p>of copyright law.</p>\n<p>The US enacted the Digital Millennium Copyright Act of 1998 and new WIPO treaties</p>\n<p>came into force i.e. the WIPO Copyright Treaty(WCT) &amp; WIPO Performances and</p>\n<p>Phonograms Treaty (WPPT).</p>\n<p>In all this, three important factors have helped preserve some balance;</p>\n<p>i.    Judicial Interpretation</p>\n<p>ii.   New Legislation</p>\n<p>iii.   Ability of copyright holders to find ways of applying the new technologies to</p>\n<p>generate sufficient returns and maintain creativity.</p>\n<p>Provisions to prohibit circumvention and anti- trafficking have alsobeen put in place to</p>\n<p>protect digital works from being infringed.</p>\n<p>As technology advances, more and more challenges are faced by the copyright system.</p>\n<p>There is need to bring about a balance of opposing interests of copyright owners and</p>\n<p>consumers. The law must create both strong incentives to create and equally strong</p>\n<p>disincentives for hackers. Technical measures are crucial to protect copyright and</p>\n<p>provide for effective means such as search engines or web crawlers to track down</p>\n<p>offenders.</p>\n<h2 id=\"international-protection-of-copyright-amp-relatedrights\">INTERNATIONAL PROTECTION OF COPYRIGHT &amp; RELATEDRIGHTS</h2>\n<h4 id=\"general-principles\">General Principles:</h4>\n<p>• National treatment</p>\n<p>• Minimum protection</p>\n<p>• Law of the country where protection is claimed</p>\n<p>• Independence of law of country origin</p>\n<p>• Dispute          settlement       under       general   rules   of   public</p>\n<p>international law</p>\n<p>Points of Attachment</p>\n<p>• Nationality</p>\n<p>• Headquarters or domicile</p>\n<p>• Place of first publication (within 30 days)</p>\n<p>• Place where physically situated</p>\n<p>• Place of performance/ recording</p>\n<p>• Place    of transmission</p>\n<p>Reproduction Rights</p>\n<p>•</p>\n<p>• Fixation</p>\n<p>• Translation, adaptation</p>\n<p>• Public performance</p>\n<p>• Broadcasting and other communication to the public</p>\n<p>• Resale right</p>\n<p>• Moral rights (authorship &amp; integrity)</p>";
